{
  "coreName": "PCSX2",
  "systemId": "PS2",
  "author": "the PCSX2 Dev Team",
  "url": "https://github.com/PCSX2/pcsx2",
  "romFile": "disc",
  "deterministic": true,
  "memoryLayoutMiB": [
    512,
    16,
    64,
    16,
    3072
  ],
  "_memoryLayoutMiB_note": "sbrk, sealed, invisible, plain, mmap. A PlayStation 2 is the biggest machine in the bundle, and most of the mmap arena is not the console: 285MB is the machine (32MB of EE RAM, the bios and rom areas, the IOP's 2MB, the vector units, and the vtlb's two page tables over a 4GB address space) and 305MB is the recompiler cache region this build reserves and never writes. The sandbox charges for pages that are TOUCHED, so most of that is address space rather than memory - but the arena has to be big enough to hold it. The mmap arena is the one that matters and the one that grew: musl sends every LARGE allocation to mmap rather than to the heap, so the machine's memory, the code region and - when the renderer setting asks for OpenGL - a whole Mesa all come out of it. Growing sbrk instead does nothing, and taking from mmap to pay for it makes startup fail earlier, which is how this was found. The whole layout has to fit inside a single 4 GiB region.",
  "video": {
    "_comment": "buffer CAPACITY; the live size comes from GetVideoWidth/Height per frame, which follow the display circuits the game programs. 640x448 is the ordinary NTSC frame, 1280x1024 the most the GS scans out.",
    "width": 1280,
    "height": 1024,
    "virtualWidth": 640,
    "virtualHeight": 448,
    "vsyncNumerator": 60000,
    "vsyncDenominator": 1001,
    "getBgra": "GetVideoBgra"
  },
  "audio": {
    "_comment": "SPU2 mixes at 48kHz, so a frame is about 800 stereo samples. The time stretcher upstream turns on by default is OFF here: a frame's samples are the ones the chip produced, and buffering is the frontend's business.",
    "samplesPerFrame": 2048,
    "channels": 2,
    "get": "GetAudio"
  },
  "lag": {
    "inputWasRead": "InputWasRead"
  },
  "extensions": {
    ".iso": "PS2",
    ".bin": "PS2",
    ".img": "PS2",
    ".chd": "PS2",
    ".cso": "PS2",
    ".zso": "PS2",
    ".gz": "PS2"
  },
  "input": {
    "name": "DualShock 2",
    "_comment": "The controller a PS2 ships with. The two sticks are axes; the Analog button is a real button on the pad, because it switches the controller between digital and analog mode and a game can see the difference. Pressure sensitivity is part of the protocol PCSX2 implements, but a frontend sends a button as pressed or not, so full pressure is what a press means here.",
    "buttons": [
      "Up",
      "Down",
      "Left",
      "Right",
      "Start",
      "Select",
      "Square",
      "Cross",
      "Circle",
      "Triangle",
      "L1",
      "R1",
      "L2",
      "R2",
      "L3",
      "R3",
      "Analog"
    ],
    "axes": [
      {
        "name": "Left Stick X",
        "min": -32768,
        "max": 32767,
        "neutral": 0
      },
      {
        "name": "Left Stick Y",
        "min": -32768,
        "max": 32767,
        "neutral": 0
      },
      {
        "name": "Right Stick X",
        "min": -32768,
        "max": 32767,
        "neutral": 0
      },
      {
        "name": "Right Stick Y",
        "min": -32768,
        "max": 32767,
        "neutral": 0
      }
    ]
  },
  "settings": [
    {
      "name": "fast_boot",
      "display": "Skip the Console Boot",
      "type": "bool",
      "default": false,
      "description": "Boot the disc's own program directly instead of watching the console start up. The bios animation is about twenty seconds of emulated time and the machine is in a different state at the end of it, so this is part of what a movie was recorded on rather than a convenience: a run made with it does not replay without it."
    },
    {
      "name": "memcard1",
      "display": "Memory Card in Slot 1",
      "type": "bool",
      "default": true,
      "description": "Whether the machine has a card in its first slot. Games ask on their first screen and behave differently without one, so this is part of the machine. The card itself is not a file: it arrives from, and returns to, the project's save data."
    },
    {
      "name": "memcard2",
      "display": "Memory Card in Slot 2",
      "type": "bool",
      "default": false,
      "description": "The same for the second slot, which a console usually has empty."
    },
    {
      "name": "rtc_year",
      "display": "Clock: Year (00-99)",
      "type": "int",
      "default": 20,
      "min": 0,
      "max": 99,
      "description": "What year the console's clock says. A PS2 has a battery-backed clock and PCSX2 would otherwise read the computer's, which makes every run a different machine - some games seed their random numbers from it, and every save is stamped with it. The default is 2020: late enough that every PS2 game accepts it as today."
    },
    {
      "name": "rtc_month",
      "display": "Clock: Month",
      "type": "int",
      "default": 3,
      "min": 1,
      "max": 12,
      "description": "The month on the console's clock."
    },
    {
      "name": "rtc_day",
      "display": "Clock: Day",
      "type": "int",
      "default": 4,
      "min": 1,
      "max": 31,
      "description": "The day on the console's clock."
    },
    {
      "name": "rtc_hour",
      "display": "Clock: Hour",
      "type": "int",
      "default": 0,
      "min": 0,
      "max": 23,
      "description": "The hour on the console's clock."
    },
    {
      "name": "rtc_minute",
      "display": "Clock: Minute",
      "type": "int",
      "default": 0,
      "min": 0,
      "max": 59,
      "description": "The minute on the console's clock."
    },
    {
      "name": "rtc_second",
      "display": "Clock: Second",
      "type": "int",
      "default": 0,
      "min": 0,
      "max": 59,
      "description": "The second on the console's clock."
    },
    {
      "name": "verbose",
      "display": "Log the Machine",
      "type": "bool",
      "default": false,
      "description": "Let PCSX2 say what it is doing on the standard error stream: which bios and disc it found, what the IOP loaded, what it refused. It changes nothing about the machine - the clock this core runs on belongs to the emulated console, not to whoever is reading the log - and it is the first thing to turn on when a project will not start."
    },
    {
      "name": "renderer",
      "display": "Renderer",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "software",
        "opengl"
      ],
      "default": "software",
      "description": "Which renderer draws the picture. Both are software and neither uses a GPU, so both are deterministic and a movie made under one replays under the other - the machine's own memory is identical either way. 'software' is PCSX2's own software renderer, and about five times faster, which is why it is the default. 'opengl' is PCSX2's OpenGL renderer, the one upstream tests and most games are looked at through, drawing through a Mesa softpipe compiled into the core. It is slower, and it is the one to try when a game looks wrong."
    },
    {
      "name": "bios",
      "display": "Boot ROM",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "ps2-0100j-20000117",
        "ps2-0101j-20000217",
        "ps2-0110a-20000727",
        "ps2-0120a-20000902",
        "ps2-0120e-20000902",
        "ps2-0120j-20001027-185015",
        "ps2-0120j-20001027-191435",
        "ps2-0150a-20001228",
        "ps2-0150e-20001228",
        "ps2-0150j-20010118",
        "ps2-0160a-20010427",
        "ps2-0160a-20011004",
        "ps2-0160a-20020207",
        "ps2-0160e-20010704",
        "ps2-0160e-20011004",
        "ps2-0160e-20020319",
        "ps2-0160h-20010730",
        "ps2-0160h-20020426",
        "ps2-0160j-20010427",
        "ps2-0170a-20030325",
        "ps2-0170e-20030227",
        "ps2-0170j-20030206",
        "ps2-0190a-20030623",
        "ps2-0190c-20030623",
        "ps2-0190e-20030623",
        "ps2-0190h-20030623",
        "ps2-0190r-20030623",
        "ps2-0200a-20040614",
        "ps2-0200e-20040614",
        "ps2-0200h-20040614",
        "ps2-0200j-20040614",
        "ps2-0210j-20040917",
        "ps2-0220a-20050620",
        "ps2-0220a-20060210",
        "ps2-0220a-20060905",
        "ps2-0220e-20050620",
        "ps2-0220e-20060210",
        "ps2-0220e-20060905",
        "ps2-0220h-20060210",
        "ps2-0220h-20060905",
        "ps2-0230a-20080220",
        "ps2-0230e-20080220",
        "ps2-0101jd-20000217",
        "ps2-0101xd-20000224",
        "ps2-0110ad-20000727",
        "ps2-0120ed-20000902",
        "ps2-0150ad-20001228",
        "ps2-0150jd-20010118",
        "ps2-0170ad-20030325",
        "ps2-0170ed-20030227",
        "ps2-0180cd-20030224",
        "ps2-0180j-20031028",
        "ps2-0250j-20100415",
        "ps2-dvd-110u",
        "ps2-dvd-120e",
        "ps2-dvd-120u",
        "ps2-dvd-130e",
        "ps2-dvd-130u",
        "ps2-dvd-200j",
        "ps2-dvd-210e",
        "ps2-dvd-210j",
        "ps2-dvd-210u",
        "ps2-dvd-212g",
        "ps2-dvd-212u",
        "ps2-dvd-213e",
        "ps2-dvd-215g",
        "ps2-dvd-300e",
        "ps2-dvd-300u",
        "ps2-dvd-302c",
        "ps2-dvd-302e",
        "ps2-dvd-302u",
        "ps2-dvd-310",
        "ps2-dvd-311"
      ],
      "default": "ps2-0230a-20080220",
      "description": "WHICH PlayStation 2 this project is. A PS2 cannot run a single instruction without its bios - there is no high-level reimplementation to fall back on - and which one it runs decides the console's region, its language, its menus, and what a game checks when it refuses to run. Each choice expects one exact dump: the firmware page names the file and the SHA1 it wants. Two projects on different bios are different machines and do not share movies. The retail consoles come first; after them the DEVKIT and debug-station roms, and last the DVD PLAYER firmware, which is the disc player's own rom rather than a boot rom - a machine will not start from one."
    }
  ],
